      "math_academy_id": "q-54472",
      "knowledge_point_id": "96967f25-565a-5338-878e-00fd30055772",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Area Bounded Below a Curve and Above the X-Axis",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038613/assets/eb35df379a8d7a20b9003218ee65e1cdcc3849af919805795dab380441d32795.png)\n\nThe area of the shaded region between the curve $y=3-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis is given by the integral expression below. What are the values of $a$ and $b?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{a}^{b}(3-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To get $a$ and $b$, we need to find the $x$-intercepts of the curve $y=3-{x}^{2}.$ \n\nWe solve the equation for $y=0$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-\\sqrt{3}$ and $b=\\sqrt{3}.$",
