      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038613/assets/d787ab7b213815ceb2cee11cf3f4a629c0f24b8328b33ae773be1e63f9f9a517.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=1$, as shown above. What is the area of the region?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{-2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
